## I

It might be useful if I started by saying a thing or two about this publication, *The Painter's Almanach.* I have talked about it a good deal before, but I'm not sure anyone has fully understood the import of this important technology, which needs to be accompanied by the *Seasons of The Heart year-long, secular liturgy* that is sprinkled throughout the *Almanach* and really brings it to life. Otherwise it would just be dusty, dried calendricals systems ("**w*****ith no sense, purpose, or meaning"***).

The calendars are super important, don't get me wrong. But what we are talking about here is what I am calling **THE BIRTH OF POLYPHONY** which I argue happened in the 8th and 9th centuries in the Carolingian Renaissance, where people still lived in ***Cyclical Time***, just before the High Middle Ages, before the clock was really a thing, at least not really (*The Birth of Mechanical Time + The Birth of Linear Chronology + The Great Synchronization*).

I believe that the *Cyclical Time* of harvests rooted in the soil, the seasons, and the repetitive liturgy of the Church, was fundamentally *monophonic* and not just in the music at the time, with its new ways of writing musical notation and whatnot, but all across the board. *I think life itself was monophonic. It was a loop; history was merely the repetition of archtetypes. And then, it was polyphonic, and today were are in a hyperreal version of this.*

## II

*The Painter's Almanach* was first designed as an actual book, but ended being scattered and distributed all over the web instead. As I said, another project, the *Seasons of The Heart* (project), is INTEGRAL to this work, which has become a daily practice.

As I have mentioned a hundred times, I am first and foremost a ***Field Artist***, I work "*in the field"* collecting data, and then once I return to my art studio + sound design/research laboratory, I turn that data into innovative cultural artifacts. ***It all has to do with the field, with fields.***

Recall that I am *an INTERDISCIPLINARY artist-researcher,* meaning that I work across Image(s), Sound(s), and Text(s). In my daily practice designed as a year-long liturgical cycle, and based in the fundamentals of *Cyclical Time*, yet adapted to 21st century realities, my work essentially becomes "*Calendar Music"* or a "*Living Historiography"*. I also suffer from synesthesia-like condition and so I paint sounds and write visual music, and every other combination you can think of. By creating music specifically to accompany the act of painting, for instance, I am creating a *Feedback Loop of Mediums*. Examples*:*

- **The Painting** records the physical gesture (*History as Space*).
- **The Music** records the temporal flow (*History as Time*).
- **The Almanac** acts as the **Chronicler.**

I cannot overstate the importance of giving anyone who reads this far yet another warning: *Do not try to launch your own Art Operation and don't try to reproduce the experiments that I talk about here or on any of the other platforms I am on.* **PLEASE HEED THIS WARNING, NOW!**

## III

This *Official Declaration of Production-Year, 2028–2029* will be short at first, but will likely have IMPORTANT UPDATES. Here are some of the things I want to talk about right now, and then reserve the right to add said UPDATES.:

- I have been working hard on formalizing all of the techniques and technologies, processes and practices, and methodologies of my *Art Operation* which "*runs"*, if you will, a piece of what I call *Cultural Software*, which is called ***The Historiotheque*** (the name of my art studio / sound design + research laboratories, *treated as software).*
- The 2028–2029 period, or "*Production-Year*," which this document announces, is going to be a crucial turning point — possibly the era when 95% of my work as an artist-researcher will be driven by machine intelligence. I saw this shift coming a decade ago when I first started the [**Schizobot system**](https://github.com/antiface/Schizobot) ("*the stupidest system in the universe*"), which was originally meant to replace me.
- I believe that I will soon begin to see the fruits of this labor, and that this disruption of the contemporary market will ripple outward, first locally, then globally. When I first started out, I harbored those grand, youthful dreams of reaching the legendary heights of a Picasso or a Dalí. While my perspective has matured, it is exactly that kind of "*holy ambition*" that has driven me on this "*sacred journey*" of creative discovery until now. I still strive for that level of excellence, come what may, though it must be said that commercial success is not my main goal.
- I realized the true meaning of the handle that I use on most of the platforms that I am on, namely, ***antisignal***. Essentially, it is a ***resynchronization signal***. People more and more today on the web and on their phones are raising the basic FREQUENCY that they run on, whereas I am trying to LOWER that same frequency as much as possible. The result is a kind of game or strategic interaction, an "***oscillation game"***, a game in the game-theoretic sense, which is why I still often call my practice "*Strategic Intermedia Art"* or else "*Tactical Media*", "*Info-Ops Art*", etc.
- I've been revisiting the hundreds of pages of notes that I took on the subject of Pablo Picasso, mostly about his *Les Demoiselles d'Avignon* painting, which many to this day still say that it "*inaugurated modern art".* I'm not sure, but who am I to argue? I just know that a significant part of any such success is luck but also obviously natural talent, and in this case I think there was a bit of the **MYSTIQUE** that he was shrouded in, perhaps to his dismay. I think though that he and George Braque had genuinely fought against the Romantic view of the artistic genius and the sort of gatekeeping "*authority"* of artists, but that this was largely ignored over the years after the fact as the mystique took over. Like it or not, though, the mystique likely contributed to his long-lasting success. But anyway originally I think Picasso and Braque had a more collaborative vision of artistic work especially in the early phases of Cubism, seeing their studios operating more as a *laboratory*. More on this later.
- In any case, I call it ***The Picasso Market.*** It's the art market of the early 20th century in Europe, but also in America. It was global, Cubism was in any case. But mostly it was European. Art historians now speak of "*transnational"* movements, meaning that they cross many borders and boundaries.
- I would love it if I could somehow avoid proving Albert-László Barabási's **First Law of Succes**s right. Realistically, I'm definitely not going to be exhibiting in any high-profile Salons around here anytime soon. But I still hold onto the hope that it's possible to achieve international acclaim without shelling out for advertising or relying on exhibits in the institutions sitting right at the center of the art world's complex network of gatekeepers.
- Barabási argues that he can predict an artist's long-term success with high accuracy just by looking at their first three exhibitions. Obviously, if you live in a major hub like Brooklyn, have the right connections, and catch a massive wave of luck, making headway is a lot easier. As I said earlier, when I first started out as a youth, I had those grand, starry-eyed aspirations of becoming the next Picasso. While I've grown a bit more grounded since then, I still strive for absolute excellence in my work, come what may.
- I want to believe I can carve out my own path without relentlessly climbing the traditional ladder — even if, deep down, expecting it to just happen feels a bit like magical thinking. But honestly, that hope is what keeps me going these days. The goal, as I see it, is to move from the periphery to the center, and I think the ultimate wild card to get there is disruptive innovation.
- I've been practing my own version of ***The Feldenkrais Method, or "Awareness-Through-Movement"***. I want to say just for a moment that I document and log everything I do online so that I can leave an audit trail of "[*breadcrumbs*](https://github.com/antiface/Documentation/tree/master/METHODS/GeneralWorkflow/Breadcrumbs)" so that people can hold me accountable, but also so I can be easily found or discovered. I have been publishing online for decades and yet I still live in obscurity as an author, a painter, a musical composer, etc. ***I predict that this will have changed by the time my Production-Year 2028–2029 comes around.***
- I also document everything so that in an ideal setting, anyone can REPRODUCE MY ART-RESEARCH EXPERIMENTS. **This is where I must give one FINAL WARNING:** *Do not run your own art-research experiments unless under supervision or else if you have reached a high level in such matters.* ***It can be extremely destabilizing. You have to be made tough.***

